WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.260
This just in: Spider-Man is set to swing back
into cinemas for an extra-special re-release.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.900
Sony Pictures announced on August 19th that
Spider-Man: Far From Home will return to theaters

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:11.080
over Labor Day weekend.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.200
Here's what you need to know about the re-release.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.120
First and most importantly is the launch date.

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:19.640
According to Variety, Spider-Man: Far From
Home's re-release run will begin on Thursday,

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.520
August 29th.

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:24.750
It's not clear how long Far From Home will
stick around in cinemas for its second outing,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.400
though it appears that it may be a one-weekend-only
engagement.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.640
Spidey fans would be wise to clear their schedules
from Thursday to Sunday just to be safe.

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.940
Sony has confirmed that those in the U.S.
and Canada will be able to attend the Spider-Man:

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.160
Far From Home re-release in standard-format
showings as well as in IMAX and large-format

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.620
screenings, available at select locations.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:46.170
Viewers might want to spend a little extra
money on an IMAX ticket for the re-release

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:50.040
of Spider-Man: Far From Home because it will
be an extended cut of the theatrical version

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:50.540
of the film.

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.020
Sony has included four minutes of additional
footage not shown in the original cut of Far

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:55.520
From Home.

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:59.940
The studio is keeping quiet about what this
extra footage entails, but we have a few ideas

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.220
regarding what it might be.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:05.920
The four minutes reportedly make up a single
scene, with Collider's Steve Weintraub speculating

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.920
that this sequence will take place ahead of
the main action of Spider-Man: Far From Home.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.030
Weintraub has good reason to suspect that,
after speaking with the director of Far From

00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:17.690
Home, Jon Watts, in a recent interview in
which Watts opened up about the scenes left

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.560
out of the theatrical cut of the movie.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:23.600
Watts explained that one deleted scene involves
Tom Holland's Peter Parker running around

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.200
New York and completing errands before jetting
off to Europe:

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.960
"There's just this very simple thing where
we had Peter doing errands, getting ready

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:30.460
for his trip.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.220
And it's going back to Mr. Delmar's, the deli,
that's been rebuilt since it was blown up

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:34.840
in Homecoming.

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.340
And he has this very Spider-Man list of things
to do.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.600
It's like get his passport, get the dual head
phone adapter so he can listen to music with

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:43.770
MJ on a flight, take down a giant gang."

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:47.510
He added that the Far From Home team put that
scene together as a kind of short film.

00:01:47.510 --> 00:01:51.860
Based on the intel Watts offered in his sit-down
with Weintraub, it seems there's a solid chance

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:55.680
that the four minutes of extra footage included
in the Spider-Man: Far From Home re-release

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.080
could be that little short film.

00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:00.500
"Keep up the good work, because I am going
on vacation."

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.090
The one question that remains after learning
all there is to know about the Spider-Man:

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.180
Far From Home re-release is why Sony is doing
it in the first place.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:11.180
While it's tough to say with complete certainty,
it appears that those at Sony are taking a

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:14.700
page out of Marvel's playbook with the Far
From Home re-release and may be looking to

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.820
push the film into an even higher tier of
financial success.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.040
Earlier this year, Marvel Studios gave Avengers:
Endgame a re-release that helped the film

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:26.200
beat out James Cameron's Avatar to become
the best-earning movie of all time.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:30.560
Far From Home, on the other hand, recently
became Sony's most profitable movie ever,

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.040
edging out Skyfall after grossing $1.109 billion
at the global box office.

00:02:35.209 --> 00:02:40.820
Far From Home is sitting atop $376 million
at the domestic box office, so it's plausible

00:02:40.829 --> 00:02:44.969
that Sony is aiming to send Far From Home
past the $400 million domestic benchmark with

00:02:44.969 --> 00:02:46.280
the upcoming re-release.

00:02:46.540 --> 00:02:50.280
In any case, the Spider-Man: Far From Home
re-release is worth getting excited about.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.980
It may not feature much extra footage, but
any time spent with the friendly neighborhood

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.740
Spider-Man is time well spent.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.960
"You're that spider-guy from YouTube, right?"

00:02:58.960 --> 00:02:59.780
"Call me Spider-Man."

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:02.360
"Okay, Spider-Man, do a flip."

